            <property key="ADM Phase" value="Phase F - Migration Planning"/>
            <property key="Artifact Type" value="Table"/>
            <property key="ArchiMate Viewpoint" value="(not a view: produced from the model)"/>
            <property key="Reference" value="TOGAF® Standard, 10th Edition - Architecture Content - https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap03.html#_transition_architecture_state_evolution_table"/>
          </element>
          <element xsi:type="archimate:ArchimateDiagramModel" name="F-M01 Business Value and Risk Matrix" id="id-8570a96ed14e495992c95e6f54c5df52">
            <child xsi:type="archimate:Note" id="id-297624ee33a84f9e90be9336ff628701" textAlignment="1" fillColor="#FFFFFF">
              <bounds x="24" y="24" width="620" height="400"/>
              <content>Business Value and Risk Matrix
TOGAF matrix - Phase F - Migration Planning

PURPOSE
Positions the work packages according to their business value and their risk, to support prioritization.

ELEMENTS (ROWS x COLUMNS)
Work Package x Value / Assessment

MAIN RELATIONSHIPS
Association, Influence

HOW TO PRODUCE IN ARCHI
Produce the matrix from the model: cross the two element types through the relationship above (File > Export > Model to CSV and a pivot table in a spreadsheet, or a jArchi script). Archi has no native matrix view.

WHERE TO STORE THE RESULT
In the Landscape model: &quot;2 Architecture Landscape&quot; (2.1 Strategic, 2.2 Segment or 2.3 Capability Architectures), or &quot;3 Solution Landscape&quot; for Solution Building Blocks. Store exported catalogs, matrices and tables with the project deliverables.</content>
            </child>
            <documentation>Guide view for the TOGAF matrix &quot;Business Value and Risk Matrix&quot; (Phase F - Migration Planning).</documentation>
            <property key="TOGAF Artifact" value="Business Value and Risk Matrix"/>
            <property key="ADM Phase" value="Phase F - Migration Planning"/>
            <property key="Artifact Type" value="Matrix"/>
            <property key="ArchiMate Viewpoint" value="(not a view: produced from the model)"/>
            <property key="Reference" value="TOGAF® Standard, 10th Edition - Architecture Content - https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap03.html#_business_value_and_risk_matrix"/>
          </element>
        </folder>
        <folder name="Requirements Management" id="id-715b4867db214d12b9ffbc0e6d83368a">
          <documentation>Guide views for the artifacts (catalogs, matrices, tables and diagrams) of the Requirements Management phase.</documentation>
          <element xsi:type="archimate:ArchimateDiagramModel" name="R-D01 Requirements Realization Diagram" id="id-e821482f91a140b7b73a34a13c73eb78">
            <child xsi:type="archimate:Note" id="id-9a7c7d6915ed46b1843f6ca62dcd20f4" textAlignment="1" fillColor="#FFFFFF">
              <bounds x="24" y="24" width="620" height="430"/>
              <content>Requirements Realization Diagram
TOGAF artifact - Requirements Management

PURPOSE
Shows how requirements and constraints are realized by core elements, and the goals and principles they derive from.

ARCHIMATE VIEWPOINT
Requirements Realization

MAIN ELEMENTS
Goal, Outcome, Principle, Requirement, Constraint, core elements

MAIN RELATIONSHIPS
Realization, Influence, Specialization

TYPICAL STAKEHOLDERS
Architecture Board, Business Owners

HOW TO USE
Use this guide as a reference. In the Landscape model, create the view in &quot;1 Architecture Requirements&quot; (1.1 Strategic, 1.2 Segment or 1.3 Capability Requirements), name it after the artifact, copy the view properties (TOGAF Artifact, ADM Phase, ArchiMate Viewpoint), set the property &quot;Architecture State&quot; (Baseline, Transition or Target) and model the content.</content>
            </child>
            <documentation>Guide view for the TOGAF artifact &quot;Requirements Realization Diagram&quot; (Requirements Management).</documentation>
            <property key="TOGAF Artifact" value="Requirements Realization Diagram"/>
            <property key="ADM Phase" value="Requirements Management"/>
            <property key="Artifact Type" value="Diagram"/>
            <property key="ArchiMate Viewpoint" value="Requirements Realization"/>
            <property key="Reference" value="TOGAF® Standard, 10th Edition - Architecture Content, §3.6.10 Requirements Management - https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap03.html#tag_03_06_08"/>
          </element>
          <element xsi:type="archimate:ArchimateDiagramModel" name="R-C01 Requirements Catalog" id="id-aa75334c113840df9519803683db701d">
            <child xsi:type="archimate:Note" id="id-3a7867e4aa274784b02f39107c7de4cb" textAlignment="1" fillColor="#FFFFFF">
              <bounds x="24" y="24" width="620" height="400"/>
              <content>Requirements Catalog
TOGAF catalog - Requirements Management

PURPOSE
Lists the requirements, assumptions, constraints and gaps of the architecture, typically one catalog per type.

MAIN ELEMENTS
Requirement, Constraint, Gap, Assessment (assumption)

MAIN RELATIONSHIPS
Realization, Specialization, Association

HOW TO PRODUCE IN ARCHI
Produce the catalog from the model: list the elements of the types above with their name, documentation and properties (model tree filter, File > Export > Model to CSV, HTML report, or a jArchi script).

WHERE TO STORE THE RESULT
In the Landscape model: &quot;1 Architecture Requirements&quot; (1.1 Strategic, 1.2 Segment or 1.3 Capability Requirements). Store exported catalogs with the project deliverables.</content>
            </child>
            <documentation>Guide view for the TOGAF catalog &quot;Requirements Catalog&quot; (Requirements Management).</documentation>
            <property key="TOGAF Artifact" value="Requirements Catalog"/>
            <property key="ADM Phase" value="Requirements Management"/>
            <property key="Artifact Type" value="Catalog"/>
            <property key="ArchiMate Viewpoint" value="(not a view: produced from the model)"/>
            <property key="Reference" value="TOGAF® Standard, 10th Edition - Architecture Content - https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap03.html#tag_03_06_08_01"/>
          </element>
        </folder>
        <documentation>Guide views for the TOGAF artifacts (catalogs, matrices, tables and diagrams), ordered by ADM phase. Prefix: &lt;phase>-&lt;type>&lt;number>, where phase is P, A, B, CD (Phase C Data), CA (Phase C Application), D, E, F or R (Requirements Management) and type is C (Catalog), M (Matrix), T (Table) or D (Diagram).</documentation>
      </folder>
      <documentation>The Reference Library provides guidelines, templates, patterns, and other forms of reference material that can be leveraged in order to accelerate the creation of new architectures for the enterprise.</documentation>
    </folder>
    <folder name="Standards Library" id="id-6238d820f56843479442609dc7e2f9ed">
      <documentation>The Standards Library captures the standards with which new architectures must comply, which may include industry standards, selected products and services from suppliers, or shared services already deployed within the organization.</documentation>
    </folder>
    <documentation>All views (diagrams) of the model.</documentation>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ArchimateDiagramModel" name="Read Me" id="id-446ae9a02f634c7395490c3d2362d5e2">
      <child xsi:type="archimate:Note" id="id-bc6d6bf84dbf474f9b15de7f2ffa93b8" textAlignment="1" fillColor="#FFFFFF">
        <bounds x="24" y="24" width="900" height="760"/>
        <content>READ ME - EA Repository Foundation (template v2.0)

PURPOSE
This model is the Architecture Foundation of the Architecture Repository (TOGAF® Standard, 10th Edition), modeled in ArchiMate® 3.2. It holds reusable, project-independent content. The enterprise landscape (Baseline, Transition and Target Architectures) lives in the companion Landscape model.

HOW TO START A NEW PROJECT
1. File > New > New Model from Template, select &quot;EA Repository Foundation&quot;.
2. Rename the model: &quot;&lt;Organization> - &lt;Project name> - Foundation&quot;.
3. Replace the placeholders in the model Purpose and in the model properties (Organization, Project, Model Owner).
4. Keep the property &quot;Template Version&quot;: it records the template release the project started from.

FOLDER STRUCTURE (identical in every layer folder and in Views)
- Architecture Metamodel: one sample element per ArchiMate 3.2 element type, grouped in one subfolder per type, and the typical relationships between them. Do not modify.
- Reference Library: reference models and Architecture Building Blocks (ABBs) reused across projects.
  Views > Reference Library > TOGAF Artifact Guides: one guide view per TOGAF artifact (catalog, matrix, table or diagram), ordered by ADM phase, with the prefix &lt;phase>-&lt;type>&lt;number> (type C = Catalog, M = Matrix, T = Table, D = Diagram). Each guide gives the purpose, the ArchiMate elements and relationships, the viewpoint or the way to produce it from the model, and the reference to the TOGAF Standard. Use them as reference when modeling views in the Landscape model.
- Standards Library: external standards and reference architectures (e.g. industry reference models).

VIEWS (folder Views > Architecture Metamodel, ordered along the TOGAF ADM)
- 00 Overview: layered view, key element types and essential relationships only.
- 01 Preliminary to 10 Phase H: element types and relationships used in each ADM phase (Phase C split into Data and Application Architecture).
- 11 Requirements Management: requirements realization and meanings.
- 99 Complete Metamodel: complete reference view, organized by layer.
Every relationship of the metamodel appears in at least one view (Archi Validator: no unused relationship).

ELEMENT AND RELATIONSHIP PROPERTIES
Lifecycle Status
Applies to: every element and relationship in the Architecture Landscape.
Purpose: states whether a concept exists today or is planned, so that Baseline, Transition and Target content can coexist in the same model.
Values:
- Planned: approved, not yet in implementation.
- In Implementation: being realized.
- Active: in operation; part of the Baseline.
- Phasing Out: retirement planned or in progress; still part of the Baseline.
- Retired: no longer in operation; kept only while referenced by a validated view, then deleted (history is preserved in version control).
Rule: the Baseline is the set of elements and relationships whose Lifecycle Status is Active or Phasing Out. Only approved content is merged into the main branch, so a work-in-progress proposal never appears with any of these values in main.

Change Reference
Applies to: every element and relationship whose Lifecycle Status is Planned, In Implementation or Phasing Out.
Purpose: traces the content to the initiative or change request that introduced or retires it.
Value: initiative name or change request identifier (e.g. initiative/&lt;name>, CR-&lt;id>).

CONVENTIONS
- All names, documentation and notes are written in English.
- View layout: within each layer, active structure elements on the left, behavior elements in the center, passive structure elements on the right (reading from left to right).
- Element names: singular, Title Case. Relationship names left empty unless they add meaning.
- Every element carries a documentation text (definition or description).
- Only ArchiMate 3.2 compliant relationships (use Archi Validator before each release).

TEMPLATE MAINTENANCE
- The master template is maintained as a regular .archimate file and exported with File > Save As Template.
- Increase &quot;Template Version&quot; and log the change in the CHANGELOG at each release.
- Existing projects can be updated with File > Import > Another Model (elements with identical IDs are updated, not duplicated).</content>
      </child>
      <documentation>How to use this template: purpose, folder structure, properties, conventions and maintenance.</documentation>
    </element>
  </folder>
  <property key="Repository Part" value="Architecture Foundation"/>
  <property key="Template Name" value="EA Repository Foundation"/>
  <property key="Template Version" value="2.0"/>
  <property key="Organization" value="&lt;Organization>"/>
  <property key="Project" value="&lt;Project name>"/>
  <property key="Model Owner" value="&lt;Name>"/>
  <purpose>Architecture Foundation of &lt;Organization> - &lt;Project name>.

Created from the template &quot;EA Repository Foundation&quot; (TOGAF® Standard, 10th Edition / ArchiMate® 3.2). It contains the reference metamodel (Architecture Metamodel), the Reference Library and the Standards Library. The enterprise landscape (Baseline, Transition and Target Architectures) is maintained in the companion model &quot;&lt;Organization> - &lt;Project name> - Landscape&quot;.</purpose>
</archimate:model>
